      "text": "To find the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle, we'll first need to identify what kind of triangle we're dealing with. Since the triangle has a side of length 6 cm and a side of length 8 cm, we can form a right triangle. This means that the hypotenuse and the longer leg is the length of the 8 cm side and the shorter leg is the length of the 6 cm side.\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem to solve for the 90-degree angle makes it easier to identify the hypotenuse of the triangle. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the hypotenuse (the longest side) is equal to the sum of the squares of the other two sides. We can represent the equation of the hypotenuse as:\n\n(8^2) - (6^2) = (hypotenuse)^2\n\nCalculating the sides, we get:\n\n64 - 36 = 28\n28 = (hypotenuse)^2\nhypotenuse = 5.2915... (to two decimal places)\nhypotenuse ≈ 5.29 cm\n\nThe shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is equivalent to the difference in the lengths of the longer and shorter sides of this triangle. \n\nThe corresponding solution to this math problem would be: \\boxed{3.82 cm}",
