      "text": "Using Pythagorean theorem, we get the equation \\[\nx^2+6^2=8^2,\n\\] where $x$ is shorter than 6, $x$ is shorter than 8, and 6 and 8 are the hypothenuses.\n\nCancelling the common factor of $6^2$ in front of the two sides, the side of length 6 can be seen as a constant 6. We end up with the equation:\n$$\n6^2+(x-6)^2=8^2.\n$$\nExpanding, simplifying, and rearranging, we get a quadratic equation\n$$\nx^2-12x+20=0,\n$$\nor $(x-2)(x-10)=0$. The only real solution of this equation is specifically $x=2$, so the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is $\\boxed{\\ 2.00}$. The answer is: 2.00",
